  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "bactria",
      "phase": "yuezhi",
      "title": "The Yuezhi reach Bactria",
      "native": "बाह्लीक",
      "when": "about 130 BCE",
      "at": 2,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          69.5,
          36.5
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "north",
          "to": "bactria",
          "traveller": "yuezhi",
          "style": "yuezhi",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "Driven out of western China by the Xiongnu, the Yuezhi, a nomad people, moved west and settled by the Oxus (Amu Darya). Here they mastered Bactria (Daxia), a land of towns once ruled by Greek kings. The Chinese envoy Zhang Qian reached them about 128 BCE; his report survives in Sima Qian’s history, the Shiji.",
      "today": "Today: northern Afghanistan, with southern Uzbekistan and Tajikistan. Balkh, ancient Bactra, lies just west of this map."
    },
    {
      "place": "kapisa",
      "phase": "yuezhi",
      "title": "Kujula unites the five",
      "native": "कपिशा",
      "when": "1st century CE",
      "at": 36,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          70.6,
          35.3
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The Yuezhi split into five chiefdoms. According to the Chinese Book of the Later Han, Kujula Kadphises, chief of the Guishuang (Kushan), conquered the other four and made himself king, then crossed the Hindu Kush and took the Kabul valley. His copper coins copy the last Greek king here, Hermaeus. His dates are disputed.",
      "today": "Today: Begram, north of Kabul, Afghanistan. The site lies beside the Bagram air base."
    },
    {
      "place": "taxila",
      "phase": "yuezhi",
      "title": "Into Gandhara",
      "native": "तक्षशिला",
      "when": "later 1st century CE",
      "at": 40,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          71,
          34.5
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "kapisa",
          "to": "taxila",
          "traveller": "kushans",
          "style": "kushan",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "Gandhara was held by Indo-Parthian kings, among them Gondophares. Kujula’s Kushans took it from them, and his coins are found in great numbers at Taxila. Some of Kujula’s coins show a head copied from a Roman emperor’s. The Book of the Later Han says he lived past eighty, and his son went on to conquer India.",
      "today": "Today: Taxila, Punjab, Pakistan, a World Heritage Site with three ancient cities: Bhir Mound, Sirkap and Sirsukh."
    },
    {
      "place": "mathura",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "Gold and Shiva",
      "native": "मथुरा",
      "when": "about 100 CE (dates disputed)",
      "at": 45,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75,
          30.5
        ],
        "width": 14
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "taxila",
          "to": "mathura",
          "traveller": "kushans",
          "style": "kushan",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "Wima Kadphises carried Kushan rule into India and struck the first Kushan gold coins. Many show Shiva with his bull, and call the king a devotee of Shiva (maheshvara). At Mat, near Mathura, a royal shrine (devakula) held statues of the kings: a seated king inscribed with the name Wima, and a headless standing Kanishka.",
      "today": "Today: Mathura, Uttar Pradesh. The statues from Mat are in the Government Museum, Mathura."
    },
    {
      "place": "rabatak",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "Kanishka’s inscription",
      "when": "about 127 CE",
      "at": 48,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          69,
          36.1
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Found in 1993, the Rabatak inscription is in Bactrian, written in Greek letters. Kanishka records his year one, names his great-grandfather Kujula, grandfather Wima Takto and father Wima Kadphises, and lists Indian cities under his rule: Saketa, Kaushambi, Pataliputra and Champa. When year one fell is disputed; 127 CE is the most widely used date.",
      "today": "Today: Baghlan province, Afghanistan. The inscription went to the National Museum in Kabul."
    },
    {
      "place": "sarnath",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "A Bodhisattva for Sarnath",
      "native": "ऋषिपतन",
      "when": "year 3 of Kanishka",
      "at": 52,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          81.2,
          26.2
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "mathura",
          "to": "sarnath",
          "traveller": "statue",
          "style": "statue",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "In Kanishka’s third year, a monk named Bala set up a tall standing Bodhisattva and a great stone umbrella at Sarnath, where the Buddha first taught. The statue is red sandstone carved at Mathura and carried over 500 kilometres east. It shows how far Kushan rule and Kushan art reached down the Ganges plain.",
      "today": "Today: Sarnath, near Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh. Bala’s Bodhisattva is in the Sarnath Museum."
    },
    {
      "place": "purushapura",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "Kanishka’s great stupa",
      "native": "पुरुषपुर",
      "when": "2nd century CE",
      "at": 54,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          72,
          34
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "At Purushapura, Kanishka built a great stupa. Chinese pilgrims, Faxian about 400 CE and Xuanzang about 630, describe it as among the tallest buildings they saw. In 1908 and 1909 excavators at Shah-ji-ki-Dheri found its relic casket. The lid shows the Buddha; the side shows a king, read as Kanishka. The inscription names Kanishka.",
      "today": "Today: Peshawar, Pakistan. The casket is in the Peshawar Museum; its relics were sent to Mandalay, Myanmar."
    },
    {
      "place": "kashmir",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "The fourth council",
      "native": "कश्मीर",
      "when": "2nd century CE",
      "at": 56,
      "source": "Tradition",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          74.5,
          34
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Buddhist tradition, recorded by Xuanzang, says Kanishka called a council of monks in Kashmir, led by Vasumitra, to settle the teaching of the Sarvastivada school; its commentaries were written on copper sheets. Another tradition places the council at Jalandhar. The poet Ashvaghosha is traditionally placed at Kanishka’s court. No inscription records the council.",
      "today": "Today: the Kashmir valley around Srinagar. Where the council met is not known."
    },
    {
      "place": "takht-i-bahi",
      "phase": "art",
      "title": "Buddhas in stone",
      "when": "1st to 3rd centuries CE",
      "at": 58,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          72,
          34.3
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Under the Kushans, sculptors made some of the earliest images of the Buddha in human form. In Gandhara they carved grey schist with Greek and Roman touches: wavy hair, draped robes. At Mathura they used red sandstone in an Indian style. Which school came first is disputed. Monasteries such as Takht-i-Bahi were filled with such work.",
      "today": "Today: Takht-i-Bahi, near Mardan, Pakistan, a World Heritage Site and among Gandhara’s best-kept monasteries."
    },
    {
      "place": "kapisa",
      "phase": "art",
      "title": "The Begram treasure",
      "native": "कपिशा",
      "when": "1st to 2nd centuries CE",
      "at": 62,
      "source": "Archaeology",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          70,
          30
        ],
        "width": 14
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "barbarikon",
          "to": "kapisa",
          "traveller": "caravan",
          "style": "caravan",
          "bend": 0.2
        }
      ],
      "text": "In 1937 to 1939, French archaeologists at Begram opened two sealed rooms full of goods: carved Indian ivories, Roman glass and bronzes, and Chinese lacquer. Kushan lands sat where trade routes from India, China and Rome met. Western goods often came by sea to Barbarikon, at the mouth of the Indus, and north from there.",
      "today": "Today: Begram, Afghanistan. Pieces are in the National Museum in Kabul and the Musée Guimet in Paris."
    },
    {
      "place": "mathura",
      "phase": "end",
      "title": "Vasudeva, the last great king",
      "native": "वासुदेव",
      "when": "about 190 to 230 CE",
      "at": 72,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.5,
          28
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "After Huvishka came Vasudeva, the first Kushan king with an Indian name. His coins show Shiva, and his inscriptions are found mostly around Mathura. In 229 CE, says China’s Records of the Three Kingdoms, a king of the Great Yuezhi named Bodiao, usually taken to be Vasudeva, sent an envoy to the Wei court.",
      "today": "Today: Mathura, Uttar Pradesh. Kushan coins and sculpture fill the Government Museum, Mathura."
    },
    {
      "place": "bactria",
      "phase": "end",
      "title": "The Sasanians take Bactria",
      "native": "बाह्लीक",
      "when": "about 230 to 260 CE",
      "at": 82,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          69.4,
          35.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "persia",
          "to": "bactria",
          "traveller": "sasanians",
          "style": "sasanian",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "In Iran a new dynasty, the Sasanians, overthrew the Parthians in 224 CE and turned east. Shapur I’s inscription at Naqsh-e Rustam claims the Kushan land (Kushanshahr) as far as Peshawar. Sasanian princes then ruled Bactria as Kushan kings (Kushanshahs). Kushan kings kept Gandhara, Punjab and Mathura for a while, in smaller and divided kingdoms.",
      "today": "Today: northern Afghanistan. Naqsh-e Rustam, with Shapur’s inscription, is near Persepolis in Fars, Iran."
    },
    {
      "place": "prayag",
      "phase": "end",
      "title": "Homage to Samudragupta",
      "native": "प्रयाग",
      "when": "about 350 to 375 CE",
      "at": 96,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          81.9,
          26
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "On the Allahabad pillar, the court poet Harishena lists the kings who offered homage to Samudragupta, the Gupta emperor. Among them is the ‘Daivaputra Shahi Shahanushahi’: titles that echo the Kushans’ ‘son of god, king of kings’, and usually taken to mean the last Kushan kings of the north-west. Soon after, the Kidarites took Gandhara.",
      "today": "Today: Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh. The pillar stands inside Allahabad Fort, closed to most visitors."
    }
  ],
